    "body": "https://github.com/cli/cli/issues/9590#issuecomment-2389674636 is closed so I'll reply here.\r\n\r\n> As far as I understand it, gh attestation verify is not a tool that that verifies SLSA provenance. If verifying SLSA provenance is what you're trying to do, the command with the --format json flag should provide the information you need. I am not an expert in this area, so if something is missing, please let us know, but we will not be adding SLSA provenance verification to the gh attestation command suite at this time.\r\n\r\nThe [readme](https://github.com/actions/attest-build-provenance/blame/dff1ae69a71254b807bee9e2f72c7ab0eed66e60/README.md#L10-L23) for actions/attest-build-provenance does say that it produces SLSA provenance and that the Github CLI is meant to verify them:\r\n\r\n> Attestations bind some subject (a named artifact along with its digest) to a [SLSA build provenance](https://slsa.dev/spec/v1.0/provenance) predicate ...\r\n...\r\nAttestations can be verified using the [attestation command in the GitHub CLI](https://cli.github.com/manual/gh_attestation_verify).\r\n\r\nYou might have been thinking of the SLSA provenances created by [slsa-framework/slsa-github-generator](https://github.com/slsa-framework/slsa-github-generator?tab=readme-ov-file#generate-provenance) reusable workflows, which  I agree are reasonably not supported by Github CLI, but can be verified instead by [slsa-framework/slsa-verifier](https://github.com/slsa-framework/slsa-verifier), according to [spec](https://slsa.dev/spec/v1.1/threats#d-external-build-parameters).\r\n\r\nRe 1:\r\n\r\nI would like for the flags not to extract information and present to the user, but to actually accept user input and verify they match the information that is in the signed provenance, particularly against the provenance's embedded certificate, whose extensions have been sourced from the reusable workflow's OIDC token. The flags could continue to be optional, but if the user-provided expected values don't match, then verification should fail.\r\n\r\nAs an example, I want to invoke the CLI with the proposed options like this:\r\n\r\n```\r\nSOURCE_REPO=\"my-org/my-source-repo\"\r\nSOURCE_REF=\"refs/heads/main\"\r\nSOURCE_COMMIT=\"abc123...\"\r\nSIGNER_WORKFLOW=\"other-trusted-org/trusted-builder-and-signer/.github/workflows/build-and-attestyml\"\r\nSIGNER_REF=\"refs/heads/main\"\r\nSIGNER_COMMIT=\"xyz456...\"\r\n\r\ngh attestation verify $ARTIFACT_PATH \\\r\n    --deny-self-hosted-runners \\\r\n    --repo \"$SOURCE_REPO\"  \\\r\n    --ref \"$SOURCE_REF\" \\\r\n    --commit \"$SOURCE_COMMIT\" \\\r\n    --cert-identity \"$SIGNER_WORKFLOW\" \\\r\n    --signer-ref \"$SIGNER_REF\" \\\r\n    --signer-commit \"$SIGNER_COMMIT\" \\\r\n```\r\n\r\nRe 2:\r\n\r\nI hope you would agree that my proposed `--jq-exit-status` option would be very useful everywhere in the CLI. My primary concern is the ability to verify source commits, source refs, signer commits, and signer refs with the CLI alone. If you were to suggest that that ability rest in my proposed `--jq-exit-status`, then I'll go ahead and open a new issue requesting that new CLI option.\r\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
