    "body": "@phillmv Thanks for looking into this. SLSA attestations are about much more than being able to validate that an artifact came from a given repository. You can summarize the spec as being about the threats and mitigation against build and distribution processes.\r\n\r\n1. Yes.\r\n\r\n    But it’s also important to have the same corresponding behavior the signing workflow. Similar to your example threat, it’s also possible that a malicious reusable workflow signs an arbitrary file. Furthermore, the malicious reusable workflow could use `actions/upload-artifact` to overwrite, or replace both the original artifact and attestation (if the attestation were also a workflow artifact). See related https://github.com/actions/download-artifact/issues/349\r\n\r\n    Currently, the attestation for this malicious artifact would have the original source repo and ref recorded in the certificate as the \"source\", but have the malicious reusable workflow's repo, filename, and ref recorded in the certificate as the \"signer\". A good defense against a consumer accidentally verifying such malicious artifacts is for the producer to instruct their users to also verify the signing repo, workflow file name, and/or commit in the attestation.\r\n\r\n    The existing `--cert-identity` [option](https://cli.github.com/manual/gh_attestation_verify) mostly enables this by allowing the user to specify a SAN regex that includes the singing repo, workflow file, and ref, like `https://github.com/myorg/my-signer-repo/.github/workflows/my-signing-workflow@refs/heads/main`.\r\n\r\n2. Propagation could be done the same way that many folks already do when they look to pin the hashes against the actions and reusable workflows in their yaml files. See [instructions](https://www.stepsecurity.io/blog/pinning-github-actions-for-enhanced-security-a-complete-guide#how-to-pin-github-actions-2). But I agree that the GitHub UI could make this flow at least one step a shorter.\r\n\r\n    Another way is that the author could publish these commit hashes to their readme on their protected main branch. That’s similar to folks publishing artifact checksums.",
